from editorial_authoring_002_184 import append
append(r'''
[141]
@Skill foundations|Food work and everyday vessels|intermediate
What knowledge did a traditional grain miller use to turn variable harvests into a usable product?|Compare grain condition, stone or mill type, intended flour, observations, maintenance and customers through a dated local practice; keep demonstrations non-operational and distinguish skill accounts from universal performance claims.|4
How can a regional food-drying tradition be documented without turning it into an unverified preservation recipe?|Trace climate, season, ingredients, labor, storage purpose and attributed household knowledge; separate historical description from present food-safety or shelf-life claims.|5
What design choices distinguish a traditional vessel for carrying water from one used for storing or cooling it?|Compare shape, weight, handles, materials, permeability, repair and local use through attributed examples; do not infer drinking-water safety from appearance or tradition.|6
[142]
[143]
@Reconstruction practice|Households, textile labor and urban exchange|intermediate
How can a Roman domestic-space reconstruction show who moved, worked and ate in different rooms?|Compare archaeological plans, objects, wear, light, water and textual accounts in a named house or settlement; distinguish modern room labels from demonstrated ancient use.|1
What work happened before a medieval wool garment reached its wearer?|Trace fiber preparation, spinning, weaving, finishing, transport, household work and specialist labor for one region and period; distinguish evidence for the labor chain from a costume-making tutorial.|2
How can an early modern urban market be reconstructed from stall rents, weights, price lists and complaints?|Follow a dated market's goods, traders, authorities, daily rhythms and excluded participants; label missing sound, smell and crowd details as reconstruction rather than eyewitness evidence.|3
@Interpretive ethics|Household inventories and missing activity|advanced
What can a historical household inventory establish about daily life and what does it omit?|Compare the document's purpose, ownership categories, debts, shared property, unpaid labor and objects not worth listing; avoid equating possessions with every activity or household member.|4
[144]
@Education history|South Asian institutions and prescriptions|intermediate
What can sources establish about a particular gurukula setting without treating every description as a universal school system?|Compare prescriptive passages, teacher-pupil relationships, patronage, access and independent historical evidence for a bounded period; distinguish ideals, attested practice and later revival claims.|1
How did learning, residence and patronage connect at Nalanda in a defined historical period?|Compare archaeological interpretation, travelers' accounts, inscriptions, curricular evidence and uncertainty; distinguish the institution's changing history from a modern university analogy.|2
@Education interpretation|Student memoirs and institutional self-image|advanced
How can a student's memoir challenge a school's official history without automatically becoming the definitive account?|Compare dates, attendance records, examinations, intended readership, recalled experience and other students' testimony; retain disagreement and limits on representativeness.|9
[145]
@Student communities|First-generation transitions and institutional scrutiny|intermediate
What can a first-generation student's first term reveal about university expectations that are rarely written down?|Trace registration, advising, housing, office hours, finances and belonging through a consented bounded account; distinguish one experience from a universal barrier list.|4
@Institutional memory|Privacy and publicly assessable outcomes|advanced
How can a campus documentary explain a student dispute without exposing unrelated education records or private peers?|Define public interest, consent, redaction, institutional power, corrections and jurisdiction/date-specific restrictions as research needs; no private records are requested or disclosed.|8
What can an accreditation or institutional-review report reveal that a university's reputation does not?|Compare the report's remit, standards, evidence, dates, conditions, follow-up and limits alongside marketing claims; distinguish accreditation from a guarantee of each student's outcome.|9
[146]
@Public influence|Named people through bounded records|intermediate
How did Rabindranath Tagore's correspondence, translations and travel shape different international readings of his work?|Build a bounded network from letters, publication and travel records; compare collaborators, audiences and translation choices without treating worldwide fame as one uniform response.|1
What do Ada Lovelace's published notes establish about her contribution and what comes from later commemoration?|Compare the original publication, technical examples, collaboration records and subsequent claims; distinguish documented text from an invented inner-life or priority narrative.|2
How can Nelson Mandela's prison letters and institutional records describe different aspects of the same period?|Compare permitted correspondence, censorship, visits, official records and retrospective accounts through a bounded interval; keep record purposes and gaps explicit.|3
[147]
@Biography evidence|Comparative lives and photograph attribution|advanced
How can a parallel biography compare two lives without pretending their opportunities and institutions were equivalent?|Define the shared question, comparison dimensions, different periods, source coverage and disconfirming cases; avoid a predetermined moral contest.|3
How can a biographer establish whether an archival photograph shows the claimed person, place and date?|Compare captions, negatives or contact sheets, provenance, publication history and corroborating records; preserve uncertain identifications and avoid identifying private living people from an image.|6
[148]
@Aid systems|Community famine-relief records|advanced
What can a famine-relief kitchen's records reveal about who received food and who was left outside?|Trace eligibility, donors, procurement, cooks, queues, local authority, household access and missing records in one dated community response; do not reduce famine to a simple shortage or a savior biography.|2
[149]
[150]
@Nomadic histories|Mobile households and taxation|advanced
How did a selected state count, tax or negotiate with pastoral households that moved across its administrative boundaries?|Compare herd assessments, seasonal registers, collectors, exemptions, mobility and pastoralists' responses in one dated jurisdiction; distinguish a taxation record from a complete picture of the community.|5
''')
